  "chooseSection": {
    "heading": "How to Choose an Empower Alternative",
    "body": "Start by identifying the primary job you want the software to do. Empower pairs free tracking tools with a wealth management advisory service. If your goal is to track your net worth, categorize transactions, and plan retirement spending, budgeting applications such as Monarch Money, YNAB and Quicken Simplifi provide specialized software. If your goal is to have someone manage your investments, automated investment managers like Betterment, Wealthfront and Fidelity Go manage portfolios for a stated fee.\n\nNext, evaluate how each service charges for its tools. Empower provides its digital dashboard tools at no cost, including Net Worth, Budgeting & Cash Flow, Retirement Planner, Portfolio Analysis, Savings Planner, Debt Paydown, Emergency Fund and Transactions. Empower earns revenue from its wealth management services, where advisory fees are calculated based on assets managed. In contrast, Monarch Money charges $99.99 per year for Core or $199.99 for Plus, YNAB charges $109 per year, and Quicken Simplifi charges $3.99 per month billed annually on promotion ($47.88 per year) or a listed $6.99 per month ($83.88 per year). When choosing tracking tools, decide whether you prefer a free platform supported by wealth management services or a paid subscription that operates without investment cross-promotions.\n\nFor investment advisory services, compare balance minimums and fee structures. Empower requires a $100,000 minimum investment for its Personal Strategy tier, which provides a dedicated financial advisor, custom portfolio construction, and tax optimization. Its Private Client tier requires $1,000,000 in investments for private equity and legacy planning. If you do not have $100,000 to invest, you cannot access Empower wealth management. Betterment offers automated portfolios for $5 a month or 0.25% annually on balances of $24,000 or with $200 monthly deposits, and requires $100,000 for its Premium tier with advisor access at 0.65% annually. Wealthfront manages portfolios for a 0.25% annual fee with no high account minimum. Fidelity Go charges a $0 advisory fee for accounts under $25,000, and 0.35% annually for accounts of $25,000 or more with one-on-one coaching included.\n\nReview the cash management terms if cash yield matters to you. Empower Personal Cash provides a standard 3.50% APY with up to $5 million in aggregate FDIC insurance and no minimums or account fees, with a 3.70% APY available for eligible Private Client members. Betterment Cash Reserve offers a base 3.50% APY, alongside a 0.75% boost for new clients with qualifying deposits up to $1,000,000, and up to $8 million in joint FDIC insurance. Neither Monarch Money nor YNAB operates a banking or cash sweep program inside its budgeting software.\n\nUse this checklist to narrow your choice:\n\n- Do you need software to track your spending and net worth, or do you need an investment manager to handle trades?\n- Do you meet Empower's $100,000 minimum for Personal Strategy, or do you prefer an advisory service with lower thresholds?\n- Would you rather pay an annual subscription for an ad-free budgeting app, or use Empower's free dashboard?\n- Do you require direct access to a human financial advisor, or does automated portfolio rebalancing meet your requirements?\n- What interest rate does your uninvested cash earn, and what is the applicable FDIC coverage limit?"
  },
  "alternatives": [
    {
      "heading": "Monarch Money Pricing and Tracking Features Compared with Empower",
      "body": "Monarch Money is a personal finance and wealth tracking subscription application. It offers two subscription tiers: Core at $99.99 per year or $14.99 per month, and Plus at $199.99 per year. A promotional code offers new users 30% off the Core annual plan for the first year. The service includes a 7-day money-back guarantee. Monarch Money states that its platform is subscription-based and ad-free, and that it does not sell customer financial data. The Plus tier adds forward-looking forecasting tools, tracking for multiple income sources such as freelance work or small business revenue, and deeper investment tracking.\n\nMonarch Money fits individuals and households that want dedicated budgeting, spending analysis, and net worth tracking without investment advisory cross-promotions or advertisements.\n\nVersus Empower, Monarch Money does not offer investment advisory services, managed accounts, or human financial planners. While Empower is an investment adviser registered with the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) managing custom portfolios, Monarch Money provides tracking software only. Empower provides its net worth and retirement tracking dashboard at no subscription cost, whereas Monarch Money requires a paid subscription after its trial window.\n\nWorked cost example: Over one year, Monarch Money Core costs $99.99 on annual billing, or $179.88 if paid monthly at $14.99 per month. Over five years on annual billing, Monarch Money Core costs $499.95. Empower's dashboard tools cost $0 for tracking net worth, portfolio holdings, and cash flow across that same five-year period."
    },
    {
      "heading": "YNAB Budgeting Method and Pricing Compared with Empower",
      "body": "YNAB is a personal budgeting application built on the principle of giving every dollar a job. It costs $14.99 per month or $109 per year, which equates to $9.08 per month on an annual schedule. YNAB provides a 34-day free trial that does not require entering payment card details. Its bank synchronization supports select financial institutions across the United States, Canada, the United Kingdom, and the European Union.\n\nYNAB fits people who want an active, forward-looking budgeting system where money is allocated into categories before it is spent.\n\nVersus Empower, YNAB focuses entirely on proactive envelope-style cash allocation rather than investment analysis or asset management. Empower tracks investment asset allocations, shows portfolio fee drag, and projects retirement readiness across all connected brokerage accounts at no charge. YNAB does not manage portfolios, does not offer investment advice, and charges $109 annually for its budgeting tools.\n\nWorked cost example: A user on YNAB pays $109 per year on the annual plan, which equals $327 over three years. Over three years, Empower charges $0 for its personal budgeting and cash flow tracking dashboard. If a user only needs after-the-fact spending categorization and net worth tracking, Empower provides that functionality without the $109 annual subscription fee."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Quicken Simplifi Cash Flow and Tracking Versus Empower",
      "body": "Quicken Simplifi is a web and mobile personal finance application that monitors spending, bills, and investments. Its pricing page shows a promotional offer of $3.99 per month billed annually for new members, alongside a regular list price of $6.99 per month. The platform includes a customizable Spending Plan, 12-month projected cash flow modeling, savings goals, watchlist categories, investment tracking, and net worth tracking.\n\nQuicken Simplifi fits budgeters who want detailed forward-looking cash flow projections, category watchlists, and net worth tracking on web and mobile.\n\nVersus Empower, Quicken Simplifi is a paid tracking tool rather than an investment advisory platform. Quicken Simplifi displays your investment holdings and performance, but it does not manage portfolios, rebalance assets, or provide access to financial advisors. Empower provides its investment analysis, retirement projection, and net worth tracking tools for free, while charging for wealth management only when clients enroll in Personal Strategy or Private Client.\n\nWorked cost example: At its promotional rate of $3.99 per month billed annually, Quicken Simplifi costs $47.88 for the first year. At its regular list price of $6.99 per month billed annually, it costs $83.88 per year, which totals $251.64 over three years. Empower provides its Net Worth, Budgeting & Cash Flow, and Retirement Planner tools for $0 across those same three years."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Betterment Advisory Fees and Cash Reserve Versus Empower",
      "body": "Betterment provides automated investing portfolios, human advisor access, and a high-yield cash account. For automated investing, Betterment charges a $5 monthly base fee, which drops to an annual fee of 0.25% when clients maintain a balance of at least $24,000 or set up recurring monthly deposits of $200 or more. Betterment Premium charges an annual advisory fee of 0.65% (a 0.25% digital base fee plus a 0.40% Premium fee) for accounts with at least $100,000, providing access to financial experts. For balances over $1,000,000, the fee steps down to 0.15% on the portion between $1 million and $2 million, and 0.10% on assets above $2 million. Betterment Cash Reserve pays a 3.50% base APY as of September 21, 2026, plus a 0.75% boost for new clients with qualifying deposits up to $1,000,000 (totaling 4.25% APY), with up to $8 million in joint FDIC insurance. Betterment supports Individual Retirement Account (IRA) transfers, 401(k) rollovers, and solo 401(k) accounts.\n\nBetterment fits investors who want automated portfolio management with published percentage pricing, as well as those with $100,000 who want access to financial advisors.\n\nVersus Empower, Betterment publishes its advisory fees directly on its website, while Empower states that fees are based on assets managed and discloses them in its Form ADV rather than as a public percentage on its website. Both services set a $100,000 minimum for human advisor access. Empower offers free tracking tools for non-clients, while Betterment charges $5 per month on investing accounts under $24,000 that lack recurring deposits.\n\nWorked cost example: On a $100,000 portfolio enrolled in Betterment Premium, the 0.65% annual advisory fee costs $650 per year ($100,000 multiplied by 0.0065). On Betterment's digital tier at 0.25%, managing that same $100,000 costs $250 per year. For Empower Personal Strategy, the annual advisory fee is calculated on assets managed per its Form ADV schedule."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Wealthfront Automated Portfolios and Pricing Versus Empower",
      "body": "Wealthfront is an automated investment platform that charges a flat 0.25% annual advisory fee on the Wealthfront Portfolio. Wealthfront's pricing page illustrates that a $15,000 portfolio costs $3.18 per month at that rate. The advisory fee covers automated portfolio management and software-based tax-loss harvesting. Wealthfront also offers a commission-free self-directed brokerage account for individual stocks and Exchange-Traded Funds (ETFs), along with cash and borrowing services.\n\nWealthfront fits investors who want automated, software-driven portfolio rebalancing and tax-loss harvesting without high minimum investment requirements.\n\nVersus Empower, Wealthfront relies entirely on software algorithms rather than human financial planners to manage portfolios. Empower's Personal Strategy pairs clients with a human financial advisor backed by planning specialists for portfolios of $100,000 or more. On the other hand, Wealthfront publishes its 0.25% annual advisory fee plainly and does not require a $100,000 minimum to manage a diversified portfolio.\n\nWorked cost example: On a $50,000 investment portfolio, Wealthfront charges 0.25% annually, which equals $125 per year ($50,000 multiplied by 0.0025). On a $100,000 portfolio, Wealthfront charges $250 per year. Empower requires $100,000 to enter Personal Strategy, where fees are calculated based on assets managed under its Form ADV."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Fidelity Go Advisory Tiers and Coaching Versus Empower",
      "body": "Fidelity Go is an automated robo-advisory service that charges tiered management fees based on account balance. Balances under $25,000 pay an annual advisory fee of $0. Balances of $25,000 or more pay an annual advisory fee of 0.35%, which includes one-on-one financial coaching sessions and automated tax-loss harvesting in taxable accounts. There is no minimum deposit required to open an account, and investing begins at $10. Fidelity Go charges no trading fees and supports 401(k) rollovers and IRA transfers.\n\nFidelity Go fits investors with balances under $25,000 seeking free portfolio management, as well as investors with $25,000 or more who want one-on-one coaching at a published 0.35% annual fee.\n\nVersus Empower, Fidelity Go allows investors to access automated management at any balance size, whereas Empower's Personal Strategy requires $100,000 to work with an advisor. For accounts under $25,000, Fidelity Go charges a $0 advisory fee, providing managed investing that Empower does not offer at that balance level. For accounts with $25,000 or more, Fidelity Go provides financial coaching at a published 0.35% rate, whereas Empower provides dedicated advisor access beginning at $100,000 with fees detailed in its Form ADV.\n\nWorked cost example: On a $20,000 portfolio, Fidelity Go charges $0 in advisory fees. On a $100,000 portfolio, Fidelity Go's 0.35% fee equals $350 per year ($100,000 multiplied by 0.0035) and includes one-on-one coaching sessions. At that same $100,000 level, Empower offers Personal Strategy with fees determined by assets managed."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Who This Is Not For",
      "body": "This comparison evaluates personal finance tracking tools and investment advisory platforms. It is not suitable for several categories of readers:\n\n- Readers seeking commercial banking or business treasury management. The tools listed here are designed for personal household finances and individual investment accounts, not corporate cash management.\n- Investors looking for self-directed day trading tools, margin speculation, or complex options trading platforms. While Empower and Wealthfront provide stock and ETF trading, they are built around long-term wealth planning rather than active trading.\n- Individuals who require formal tax preparation or certified legal advice for trust administration and estate execution. Empower offers tax filing via its dashboard, and its Private Client tier covers tax and legacy planning for $1,000,000+ balances, but digital tracking software cannot replace a qualified tax accountant or estate attorney for complex situations.\n- Anyone who does not want to connect bank or brokerage accounts digitally. All of the platforms evaluated rely on electronic account linking to monitor transactions and aggregate balances."
    },
    {
      "heading": "What Would Change Our Answer on Empower Alternatives",
      "body": "The comparison on this page reflects published terms and pricing checked on November 18, 2026. Several potential moves by these providers would change how they line up:\n\n- If Empower publishes a standard advisory fee percentage for Personal Strategy directly on its website, readers could compare its cost against Betterment, Wealthfront and Fidelity Go without reviewing its Form ADV.\n- If Empower introduces an advisory tier below its current $100,000 threshold, it would compete directly with Fidelity Go and Wealthfront for investors with smaller portfolios.\n- If Betterment modifies its $5 monthly base fee on accounts under $24,000, it would change the entry-level comparison for new investors.\n- If Fidelity Go alters its $25,000 threshold for free management or changes its 0.35% fee tier, the cost calculation for mid-sized portfolios would shift.\n- If Monarch Money, YNAB or Quicken Simplifi adds automated portfolio management or native high-yield cash accounts, the boundary between budgeting software and investment platforms would blur.\n- If Empower Personal Cash or Betterment Cash Reserve adjusts its base APY, the cash return comparison would require recalculation."
    }
  ],
  "stillBetterFit": {
    "heading": "When Empower Is Still the Better Fit",
    "body": "Empower remains the stronger fit for individuals who want robust, free tracking tools without paying a monthly or annual software subscription. Its free dashboard provides Net Worth tracking, a Retirement Planner, Budgeting & Cash Flow, Portfolio Analysis, a Savings Planner, Debt Paydown tracking, an Emergency Fund tracker, and Transaction tracking. Empower also provides a free portfolio analysis on its wealth management page with no obligation to enroll in paid advisory services.\n\nEmpower is also well suited for investors with $100,000 or more who want one-on-one guidance from a dedicated financial advisor backed by planning specialists. Its Personal Strategy provides customized portfolio management and tax optimization. For high-net-worth investors with $1,000,000 or more, its Private Client tier adds premium planning, private equity access, and legacy planning.\n\nAdditionally, Empower Personal Cash offers an attractive cash option with no account fees, no minimum deposit requirements, direct deposit support, aggregate FDIC insurance up to $5 million, and a 3.50% standard APY (or 3.70% APY for eligible Private Client customers). Investors who hold workplace retirement plans administered by Empower can also view their employer plans alongside their personal accounts under a single login."
  },
  "verdict": "Empower provides free personal tracking tools paired with human wealth management for portfolios of $100,000 or more, with advisory fees detailed in its Form ADV. If you only want budgeting and net worth tracking, Monarch Money ($99.99 per year), YNAB ($109 per year) and Quicken Simplifi ($3.99 per month billed annually on promotion) offer dedicated software tools without wealth management cross-promotions. If you want managed investing, Betterment (0.25% or 0.65% with advisors), Wealthfront (0.25%), and Fidelity Go ($0 under $25,000; 0.35% at $25,000 or more) provide clear, published percentage fees. Identify whether you need software tracking or asset management, check your portfolio balance against account minimums, and select the service that matches your operational needs.",
